  "grammar_explanations": [
    {
      "slug": "going to: affirmative for plans",
      "title_form": "Going to: afirmativo para planes",
      "title_target": "Going to: affirmative for plans",
      "description_md_form": "Usamos **going to** para hablar de **planes e intenciones** para el futuro. La estructura es: **sujeto + verbo *to be* (am/is/are) + *going to* + verbo en infinitivo**.\n\nEjemplos:\n- `I am going to send an email.` = Voy a enviar un correo electrónico.\n- `She is going to get married.` = Ella se va a casar.\n\nEs muy común en conversaciones diarias para decir lo que **ya decidimos** hacer. No lo usamos para predicciones sin evidencia (eso es *will*, que veremos más adelante).\n\n**Puntos clave:**\n- El verbo *to be* cambia con el sujeto: `I am`, `you are`, `he/she/it is`, `we are`, `they are`.\n- El verbo principal **no cambia**: siempre va en infinitivo (sin *to* extra).\n- En español, solemos decir \"voy a + infinitivo\", igual que en inglés.",
      "description_md_target": "We use **going to** to talk about **future plans and intentions**. The structure is: **subject + verb *to be* (am/is/are) + *going to* + base verb**.\n\nExamples:\n- `I am going to send an email.`\n- `She is going to get married.`\n\nIt is very common in daily conversation to say what we **have already decided** to do. We do not use it for predictions without evidence (that is *will*, which we will see later).\n\n**Key points:**\n- The verb *to be* changes with the subject: `I am`, `you are`, `he/she/it is`, `we are`, `they are`.\n- The main verb **does not change**: it is always in the base form.\n- In Spanish, we often say \"voy a + infinitive\", just like in English.",
      "grammar_structure": "Subject + to be (am/is/are) + going to + base verb",
      "conjugation_table": {
        "name": "going_to_affirmative",
        "headers": [
          "Pronombre",
          "Forma con going to",
          "Ejemplo"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "I",
            "am going to",
            "I am going to text you."
          ],
          [
            "You",
            "are going to",
            "You are going to make friends."
          ],
          [
            "He/She/It",
            "is going to",
            "She is going to get married."
          ],
          [
            "We",
            "are going to",
            "We are going to solve this problem."
          ],
          [
            "They",
            "are going to",
            "They are going to send an email."
          ]
        ]
      },
      "examples": [
